# `Orderable`

`Orderable` says a value carries an order of its own: for any two values of the
type, which comes first can be decided without being told. It is a property of
the **element**, never of a collection holding it.

```hql
abstract type Orderable

type Int     : {Scalar, Orderable}
type Float   : {Scalar, Orderable}
type Decimal : {Scalar, Orderable}
type String  : {Scalar, Orderable}
type Date    : {Scalar, Orderable}

abstract type Doc : Orderable { … }   // ordered by its name
```

It is an [[type-system|abstract type of law]], declared in
[`std/core.hql`](../../../../std/core.hql). Sorting expects the order to be total.

## Order is not position

Four names touch order, and they answer two different questions:

| Name | What it says | Whose property |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Set<T>` | membership only; no first element | the collection |
| `Seq<T>` | every element occupies a position | the collection |
| `List<T> : Seq<T>` | the elements are held, not produced | the collection |
| `Orderable` | the value has an ordering key of its own | the element |

A `Seq<T>` has positions whether or not `T` is `Orderable`: a list of booleans
has a first element, and booleans have no order. An `Orderable` element in a
`Set` still has no position. The collection names are argued on
[[hql/collections]]; this card owns the element side.

## What carries an order

| Type | Its key |
| --- | --- |
| `Int`, `Float`, `Decimal` | the number; an `Int` and a `Decimal` compare by value |
| `String` | the text |
| `Doc`, and so every `Card` | its name |
| `Hit<T>` | best score first, then the card's name |

`Bool`, `Data` and `Map` carry none. `Data` is a union of leaves, sequences and
maps, and a leaf `1` beside a leaf `"owl"` has no answer to which comes first.

A card's key is its own name, not something an extension supplies, which is why
`Card : Orderable` is ancestry rather than a capability — see [[type-system]].

## What it buys: a reproducible prefix

A `Set<Card>` has no positions. Taking a prefix of it is still reproducible,
because each card brings its key:

```hql
Set(cards) | take(2) | map(c => c.name)   // the first two names, every run
```

`cards` itself is a `Seq<Card>`, so `take` over it keeps the positions of the
sorted walk and needs no `Orderable` element — see [[card-type]].

`take` on a collection without positions requires `Orderable` elements, and so
does `sort` without a `by` argument. Either is refused otherwise, at check time:

```text
type error: `take` needs elements with an order of their own, and Bool has none; sort with an explicit key first
```

Supplying an order from outside — `sort(by = c => c.title)` or a comparison
`(a, b) => compare(…)` — is a different signature, not this one narrowed. The
key a selector returns must be `Orderable`; the element need not be.

## A ranking's tie rule

A `Hit` orders by score, best first, and two equal scores fall back to the
card's name. That is what makes a [[hql/semantic-search|ranking]]
deterministic: the order comes from the hits themselves, so the same query over
the same vault returns the same list.

Implemented by `Value::order_key` in `src/types/value.rs`.
